| Lock SHA-256 | `ab5bd11c811aa107c8d041b51f2ee865b5ac5858e12d840e0267ed9b67262de1` | `7446397d731d428b59a0633d809fe5b3ff0baa8da6c7131b2e9f09892ebd6f3b` |
| Audit status/schema | 1 / 2, valid JSON | 1 / 2, valid JSON |
| Unique advisories | 2 Moderate | Same 2 Moderate |
| Propagated entries / affected installed nodes | 40 / 42 | 40 / 42 |
| `npm ls --all --json` status | 0 | 0 |
| Direct runtime target | Red: `^9.0.9`, 9.0.9 | Pass: `^11`, 11.0.7 |
| Runtime advisory absence | Red: direct/inherited 9.0.9 affected | Pass: no affected minimatch on either runtime traversal |

Both audit runs use `npm audit --package-lock-only --json --ignore-scripts`, capture stdout,
stderr and status separately, and validate schema, vulnerability map and advisory objects.
Neither has a registry/error object; status 1 is valid findings. Malformed JSON, unsupported
schema and a simulated network-error object are rejected by the acceptance checker.
`npm explain minimatch glob --json`, lock edges and installed manifests agree with the
graph. The baseline inventory matches the historical 003 candidate's two identities and
40 entries / 42 nodes. The fresh candidate's advisory objects, affected versions and node
set are unchanged; no advisory is added, withdrawn or changed. The outcome is removal of
runtime reachability, not a lower repository finding count.

Actual installed runtime edges:

- Baseline CLI 18.4.1 → llmist `^18.4.1` → workspace llmist 18.4.1 → minimatch `^9.0.9`
  → `node_modules/minimatch@9.0.9`, inside `>=7.0.0 <11.0.1`.
- Candidate CLI uses the same llmist edge; llmist → minimatch `^11` now resolves to
  `packages/llmist/node_modules/minimatch@11.0.7`. Traversal of actual dependencies and
  optional dependencies from both consumers finds no minimatch version in that advisory
  range. Workspace dev flags are not used to classify these paths.

The ordinary npm lock write changes exactly five entries: llmist's dependency range plus
four new required package entries. It leaves every other lock entry byte-equivalent as JSON.

| Required entry | Version / reason |
|---|---|
| `packages/llmist` | Only `dependencies.minimatch`: `^9.0.9` → `^11` |
| `packages/llmist/node_modules/minimatch` | 11.0.7, direct runtime target; root 9.0.9 remains for glob/TypeDoc |
| `node_modules/@isaacs/nonrecursive-glob-match` | 1.1.0, new `^1.1.0` matcher dependency, BlueOak-1.0.0 |
| `packages/llmist/node_modules/brace-expansion` | 5.0.12, required `^5.0.12`, MIT |
| `packages/llmist/node_modules/balanced-match` | 4.0.4, brace-expansion's `^4.0.2`, MIT |

There is no workspace version/metadata normalization, unrelated update, forced resolution,
bundler edit or manually rewritten lock. npm ci enforces transitive engine compatibility.

### Characterization, mutation controls and built consumers

Before the manifest edit, all 41 tests across the three touched test files pass against
9.0.9, including the fourteen plan matrix cases, five additional default-edge cases and
the built consumers. After upgrade/rebuild, all 183 tests in the eleven library/CLI skill
files pass with the same assertions. Baseline and candidate builds both pass.

Temporary production mutations independently demonstrate right-reason assertion failures:
return no selection (top-level/globstar); select all (nonmatch); check only the first
pattern (later alternative); collect per-pattern results (uniqueness); match missing and
empty patterns (separate cases); include model-disabled skills; enable dot/nocase or
disable brace/extglob/negation; and expand the segment boundary to globstar. All fourteen
matrix cases fail at their named selection assertion under the relevant mutation. Empty
paths use explicit YAML `[]` and assert parsed metadata, distinct from absent paths.

Built ESM and CommonJS tests spawn real Node processes resolving `llmist` package exports
from its workspace, outside Vitest's source alias. Each asserts exact selected names,
read instructions, activated skill name and resolved instructions with shell preprocessing
disabled, as well as zero exit and empty stderr. Independent fixture-name and instruction
mutations fail both tests; invalid export paths fail their parent exit-status assertions.

The built CLI test spawns the absolute CLI entry from a normal isolated temporary project,
discovers its uniquely named `.llmist/skills` fixture, lists it, and verifies info's name,
paths, source and exact instruction section. Different fixture names and instruction text
each fail the parent assertion. Fixtures are cleaned in `finally`; child timeouts are
bounded and neither `HOME` nor `TMPDIR` is overwritten. All temporary mutations are restored
and the three touched files return to 41 passing tests before the dependency edit.
No existing assertion is removed or narrowed; no provider or exploit calls are made.

### Residual reconciliation — unresolved — upstream-only

G = **GHSA-3g3m-2fjr-6pw4**, Moderate, glob `>=9.0.0 <=14.0.1`; fixed boundary 14.0.2.
M = **GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249**, Moderate, minimatch `>=7.0.0 <11.0.1`; fixed boundary 11.0.1.
The fresh advisory ranges and semver checks place the fixed versions outside the following
parent ranges. Every path below remains unresolved — upstream-only, without suppression,
accepted-risk claim or exception. Reachability proves neither exploitability nor safety.

- **Development cleanup:** root and llmist/CLI/testing dev manifests → rimraf `^5.0.5`
  (5.0.10) → glob `^10.3.7` (10.5.0, G) → minimatch `^9.0.4` (root 9.0.9, M).
  Both fixed boundaries require upstream parent changes beyond this direct runtime scope.
- **Private documentation:** docs → TypeDoc `^0.28.15` (0.28.17) → minimatch `^9.0.5`
  (root 9.0.9, M). Docs → starlight-typedoc `^0.22.0` (0.22.0) peers TypeDoc `>=0.28.0`
  and typedoc-plugin-markdown `>=4.6.0`; docs also declares that plugin `^4.9.0` (4.10.0),
  which peers TypeDoc `0.28.x`. These peers reach the same affected TypeDoc dependency.
  Upstream TypeDoc/plugin changes are outside scope; private docs are not shipped runtime.
- **Release tooling:** root → semantic-release `^25.0.2` (25.0.3) → @semantic-release/npm
  `^13.1.1` (13.1.5) → npm `^11.6.2` (11.21.2). Actual bundled manifests retain glob
  13.0.6 (G) and minimatch 10.2.5 (M): npm declares `^13.0.6` / `^10.2.5`; bundled glob
  declares minimatch `^10.2.2`. Map-workspaces/package-json/cacache glob ranges are `^13.0.0`;
  arborist/map-workspaces/ignore-walk/libnpmdiff minimatch ranges are `^10.0.3`, and
  @tufjs/models uses `^10.1.1`. Neither fixed major is within those examined ranges.
  Only an upstream release-tool bundle update can change these; no bundle edits are made.

The complete affected-node table retains immediate installed parents and their ranges,
including peer edges and cycles. Composing these edges with the root/workspace chains above
gives actual full parent chains. `N/` means `node_modules/`; `B/` means
`node_modules/npm/node_modules/`. All `B/` nodes and the semantic-release/plugin chain are
release tooling; rimraf/glob/root minimatch are cleanup, and TypeDoc/plugin paths are private
docs. Propagated entries carry G/M from their reachable advisory object, not a separate GHSA.

